        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Danish PM secures third term despite party's historic setback Europe Issued on: 25/03/2026 - 12:24 Modified: 25/03/2026 - 12:41 Social Democrat Mette Frederiksen has said she is \"ready to assume\" the role of Prime Minister of Denmark again, despite her party's significant setback in March 25's parliamentary elections, which saw the left-wing bloc win the most votes but lack a majority. With 21.9% of the vote, the Social Democrats reached their lowest level since 1903, far from the 27.5% they achieved in 2022. Zelensky slams 'absolute depravity' after massive Russian drone attack 25/03/2026 Europe Russia and Ukraine escalate drone attacks as Moscow starts spring offensive 25/03/2026 Europe Denmark's Frederiksen weakened by election setback, coalition talks loom 25/03/2026 Europe Hungary is 'the Kremlin's biggest agent in the EU', analyst says 24/03/2026 Europe Danish PM's left bloc leads election, but no majority 24/03/2026 Europe Russia rains drones on Ukraine, killing eight, hitting UNESCO site in Lviv 24/03/2026 Europe Danish PM seeking third term as election underway 24/03/2026 Europe EU and Australia sign free trade deal and boost defence cooperation 24/03/2026 Europe Denmark election dominated by domestic issues followed by Greenland crisis 24/03/2026 Europe"
